/// NODE VARIABLES — a node declares a named choice, a descendant reads it.
///
/// The declaration is what makes the reference a real edge rather than a string
/// two places happen to spell the same. A reader with no declaring ancestor is a
/// build error naming both; resolution is by NAME up the ancestor chain, so a
/// node that declares nothing is transparent and a nested declaration of the
/// same name shadows for its own subtree.
///
/// The one place this is load-bearing rather than tidy: a query parameter read
/// from a variable is a per-viewer question the SERVER must answer before it
/// renders, because the first HTML a browser gets is complete. A value matched
/// by convention in the browser cannot be.
///
/// ## Declaring one
///
/// A declaration is a NAME and the value it holds before anybody chooses — so
/// it is written directly, with no wrapper to learn:
///
///     (c.column) {
///       vars { ["window"] = "24h" }
///       children { c.historyChart(sensor, varMod.ref("window")) }
///     }
///
/// There is deliberately no "set of allowed values" field beside it. What a
/// variable may hold is decided by whoever READS it — a chart's provider
/// refuses a window it cannot parse, and the write boundary refuses a value no
/// declared reader can use — so a list here would be a second, weaker copy of
/// that, free to disagree with the control the author actually rendered.
///
/// Where a closed set of values IS the point, it is PKL's job and belongs in
/// one place: a function that emits the declaration and the control it belongs
/// to from the same list, so they cannot drift.
///
///     const function windowControl(name: String, choices: Listing<String>,
///                                  start: String) = …
module core.variable

/// A reference to a declared variable (fh.view.model.Ref.Var), for the places
/// that take `String|Ref` — a value written down, or a read.
///
/// Backticked because `var` is the WIRE key and reads as one word there. Nobody
/// writes this class directly; [[ref]] is the spelling.
class Ref {
  `var`: String
}

/// Read the variable `name`, wherever it is declared above this node.
///
/// Named `ref` and not `read`: `read` is a Pkl KEYWORD (the resource reader),
/// and using it as a function name is a parse error pointing at the definition
/// rather than at the call.
const function ref(name: String): Ref = new Ref { `var` = name }
